{"kind":"request","text":"Restore a genuinely fast default correctness suite and give it a generous, stable package ceiling without turning wall-clock latency into a correctness assertion. A 2026-08-11 uncached go test -short run on the current main measured internal/kernel at about 113 seconds and internal/app at about 40 seconds; the next package was 3.7 seconds, and the largest individual tests were about 21.7, 18.4, and 4.6 seconds. These are diagnostic point measurements, not a portable baseline. Find the expensive fixture construction and repeated real-Git work in the ordinary kernel and app tests. Preserve important audit, checkpoint, corruption, authority, and fallback correctness coverage with the smallest fixtures that exercise each boundary; share or restructure safe setup where that makes the suite cheaper. Move only genuine scale, load, latency-distribution, and soak scenarios to benchmarks or an explicit performance build tag. After ordinary packages normally finish with ample headroom, make the default test command use Go short mode and a 30-second per-package timeout. Produce a non-gating slowest-package and slowest-test report from the same go test JSON stream used by CI, so approaching the ceiling is visible without executing the suite again. Coordinate with #2920, which already removes duplicate CI executions, and do not replace its duplication with a timing-specific rerun. Do not add time.Since latency assertions inside tests and do not set a loose 120-second ceiling that merely preserves the current failure mode.","body":{"branch":"request/fast-test-lane","conditions":"The ordinary correctness suite retains direct coverage of security, audit, checkpoint, corruption, authority, and honest-fallback boundaries; every case moved to the performance lane is identified in the report and either is genuinely about scale or latency or keeps a smaller correctness counterpart in the default lane; expensive kernel and app fixture costs are measured, explained, and reduced so each ordinary package normally completes within 10 seconds on the recorded reference runner; the default command uses short mode and a 30-second per-package timeout only after that headroom exists; benchmarks and tagged performance scenarios are not run by make test or the ordinary every-change review; CI reports the slowest packages and tests from its existing go test JSON stream without a second execution and coordinates with #2920; no individual test asserts a small elapsed-time budget; cached local runs remain available while the recorded CI timing run is honest about cache state; make test, race, vet, build, spike evidence, and relevant mutation checks pass; implementation is on the named request branch and receives independent exact-head review before merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}